ACE: Not bad at all...
Tyrone: Heh... I actually understood that one.
Tyrone: But I'm wondering what Java really did with all those boxes.
ACE: Here's the play by play. Java made a box named x and dropped the number 5 inside.
ACE: Then it made a box named message and dropped the text Hello World inside.
ACE: Then the if statement asked a question. Is message the same as Hello World?
Tyrone: And it was, so the answer was true.
ACE: Right! So Java ran the first block and printed message. The else block, the one that says Try again, never ran.
Tyrone: So it only ever takes one road.
Tyrone: Two equals signs ask, one equals sign stores. I'll remember that.
ACE: Exactly. And every name has to match its box, letter for letter. message is not the same as Message.
ACE: Small things like that are where most slips happen, so it's good you noticed them.
Tyrone: One wrong letter and Java has no idea what I mean.
Tyrone: Something tells me the next one is where it really gets scary...
ACE: But you're still learning, aren't you?
